From d5dca4289ae1ef2dc16444f53954884cae0a65d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 18:29:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] vulkan/wsi: Horizon, fall back to the synchronous dequeue
 mode
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 7 (nwindowDequeueBuffer / nwindowQueueBuffer)
Why: measured on hardware, on the window that had just failed, twice in
 one run:

   2 buffers, interval 1: asked for 5000 ms — 78166 dequeue(s) in
   libnx's mode, release event fired 78165 time(s), last result
   0x0000115d
   2 buffers, interval 1: async=false returned 0x00000000 after 145 us

 async=true answers NO_INIT for as long as you care to ask; async=false
 hands over a buffer immediately, and does not block inside the
 compositor — the one behaviour that could not be ruled out until it was
 tried.

 The reading: Android's producer takes `async` to mean "this producer is
 in asynchronous mode", where queueBuffer never blocks and older frames
 are dropped, which costs the queue one buffer held in reserve. A
 two-buffer queue with both buffers out cannot spare it. FIFO
 presentation is synchronous by definition, so async=false is the mode
 that describes what a swapchain is doing. Every other observation fits:
 three buffers usually have a free slot and answer async=true fine, and
 a producer that outruns the display drops a frame, frees a slot, and
 also answers async=true fine — which is why this only ever showed up at
 two.

What: the acquire asks async=true first, because that is libnx's path
 and the common case, and falls back to async=false in the same
 iteration when the queue says it would block.

 Not asked when the caller passed a zero timeout. async=false is the
 mode Android permits to block inside the server; it did not on the
 window measured, but vkAcquireNextImageKHR(timeout = 0) must answer
 VK_NOT_READY promptly and that promise is not worth risking on one
 measurement.

 This is not patch 0061 again. 0061 had the right mode and used it
 wrongly: async=false was tried only after an eventWait that had already
 consumed the whole timeout, and async=true was never asked again. Both
 modes are now asked every iteration, and 0062's slicing keeps any one
 call from eating the deadline.

Cross build: green. Not verified on hardware.
---
 src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
index 47213b0..7f68685 100644
--- a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
+++ b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
@@ -1050,6 +1050,28 @@ wsi_horizon_acquire_zero_copy(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
       Result rc = wsi_horizon_try_dequeue(chain, false /* libnx's mode */,
                                           &slot, &fence);
 
+      /* THE SECOND MODE, AND IT IS WHAT MAKES TWO IMAGES WORK.
+       *
+       * Measured on hardware (2026-08-05) on a two-buffer window with
+       * both buffers queued: async=true answered NO_INIT 78166 times
+       * running, and async=false handed over a buffer in 145 us without
+       * blocking. Android's producer reads `async` as "this producer is
+       * in asynchronous mode" — queueBuffer never blocks and older
+       * frames are dropped — which costs the queue one buffer held in
+       * reserve. A two-buffer queue with both buffers out cannot spare
+       * it. FIFO is synchronous by definition, so async=false is the
+       * mode that describes what a swapchain is doing.
+       *
+       * Not asked when the caller passed a zero timeout. async=false is
+       * the mode Android allows to block inside the server; it did not
+       * on the window measured, but vkAcquireNextImageKHR(timeout = 0)
+       * must answer VK_NOT_READY promptly and that promise is not worth
+       * risking on one measurement.
+       */
+      if (wsi_horizon_dequeue_would_block(rc) && timeout_ns != 0)
+         rc = wsi_horizon_try_dequeue(chain, true /* FIFO's own mode */,
+                                      &slot, &fence);
+
       if (R_SUCCEEDED(rc)) {
          struct wsi_horizon_image *image = &chain->images[slot];
 
-- 
